A||||15||Wolfhound||Atlanta Trip||||1||
Z||000000||Wolfhound||03-23-2001||06:27 AM||||Getting a Fiero out of Atlanta is a trip. On the way I saw this truck aproaching from my rear. Are they getting more aggresive or what! Note the teeth on the semi!<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/bd_1.JPG">   <BR>When I got to the car, there was no one to help me load it on the trailor, so it was a slow process alternating a come-a-long and the park possition, but it worked.<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/gone_1.JPG">   <BR>Let me tell you getting the car out of Atlanta is nerve racking. Pulling onto 85 South is like leaving the pits at Indy. you had better be doing 70 when you leave the on ramp and just hope there is an opening.<BR>The trip was uneventfull until I was about 10 miles from home and one of the trailor tires had a tread explode. I pulled off and was surprized to find it was still holding air and managed to make within 20 feet of my house before it gave up the ghost.<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/homi.JPG">   <BR>I haven't had a chance to check the engine yet. I,ll change out the steering column today and see if it runs. A sticker on the window says the oil was changed less than a month ago! Fingers crossed.  <P>[This message has been edited by Wolfhound (edited 03-23-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Wolfhound (edited 03-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||RossT||03-23-2001||06:57 AM||||Now that you have another fiero, you can sell me that blue one!!!<P>Thats one thing I notice about driving a fiero is that it always seems that someone is on your as$..<P>nice find btw.<p>[This message has been edited by RossT (edited 03-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wolfhound||03-23-2001||07:01 AM||||Fat Chance!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> This is no.14||||reg||1
Z||000003||RossT||03-23-2001||07:04 AM||||#14, come on, you could part with one of them. you can only drive one at a time!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wolfhound||03-23-2001||07:49 AM||||Five are parts cars, but they sure have been handy to have around. I enjoy working on them almost as much as driving them. <BR>The blue GT is my Best and will be around for a while.<BR> I can usually find a whole car for less than the cost of parts I need or find a deal like this I just can't pass up, quanity was never the intent.<BR> Part of the beauty of the Fiero is the fact so much is interchangable and they are relativly inexpensive now. Also the more I work on them the easier and quicker it is.  There's still a lot I don't know about them but the mystery is deminishing rapidly.<BR> Look at what I found under the tacky streering while cover shown in the insert. Leather and screaming perfect, worth the price of the car<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tack.JPG">  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Raydar||03-23-2001||09:17 AM||||Wolfhound,<BR>Congrats on your find! <BR>Glad you survived Atlanta. We <I>can't</I> drive. <BR>It's a normal thing in the morning to be doing 75-80, with traffic (speed limit is 65, between home and city), and have some moron right on your ass, wanting to get around, even though there's no place to go. <BR>The 18-wheeler with the teeth reminds me of Stephen King's Maximum Overdrive.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 03-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Wolfhound||03-23-2001||09:33 AM||||Yeah, The teeth were cool, But last thing I needed to see before the haul. Kept me on my toes though. I can handle Atlanta under normal conditions, but my trailor was a little doubtful to start with. That would have been a good shot out of the back of a Fiero and closer.||||reg||1
Z||000007||elfiero||03-23-2001||09:36 AM||||Looking at those pics,you need some banjo<BR>pick'in in the backround.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Wolfhound||03-23-2001||10:46 AM||||Got it elfiero. Garrison Kellor and friends from Lake Wobegon where all the Women are good looking and all the Children above average.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Wolfhound (edited 03-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Wolfhound||03-23-2001||03:27 PM||||Changed out the steering colume and she tried to crank. Noticed fuel level below zero. Drove to a nearby store to get gas. Add 2.5 galleons to 2.5.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> not only does it run it runs good and quiet. OK! Time to check the tranny out. Close doors trunk and hood. (Uh Oh) it won't come out of park. Came inside and started to post in the tech section, but decided to check theogre Cave.<BR>Good move! Dropped the column again and checked the park lock. The pin that slides into the switch is curved. Man they tried hard to steal this car. Theogre explanation regarding changing the end mechanism saved me taking out the cable. Took it for a spin down the drive and the 1/2 mile dead end dirt road I live on.  Tranny wouldn't lock?  When I got to Hy 81 I checked the connection to the lockup. Unplugged. Another defeat that didn't work. Long reply Huh! Oh Thanks Ogre! you continue to be an asset to the forum.<BR>Coy<BR> ||||reg||1
